<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<rules version="3">
    <!-- Every rule is a <rule>. Load this base, then rules_<language>.xml and rules_<language>_<COUNTRY>.xml
         (SearchLocales: the locale of the map data, "en_US" for a US map, "de_CH" for name:de of a Swiss map).
         This base holds only rules that are true in every language: today there are none, words and their
         abbreviations belong to a language file. A map without a locale ("" - World basemap, an unknown map)
         gets no word rules at all.

         Where a rule stands tells where it works:
           <index>  the OBF writer adds the form to the names of objects, once, with the locale of the map and of
                    the name tag (name:de on an Italian map is de_IT). The result is stored in the OBF.
           <query>  the search applies the rule to a query word, with the locale of every OBF it reads.
           <rules>  (a rule outside <index> and <query>) both sides; the rule is a pair of plain words.
         A pair of forms is matched on one side only: query when one word expands to one word (or to alternative
         words), index when the form is a phrase, a glued or tokenizer-lost form.

         Attributes:
           from     a plain word (matched as the whole word, ignoring case) or a Java regexp.
           to       the replacement ($1, etc. for a regexp). Several rules with one from are alternatives: the query
                    word matches any of them. In <query> and <rules> it is one word. An empty to in <query> makes
                    the word ignorable: a conjunction or an article, it is not an unmatched word of a name.
           object   the owner of the name: street, locality, boundary, postcode, poi, "a,b" for several, "*" (the
                    default) for any; "building" in <query> marks the word as a part of a house number (no to).
           mode     All replaces all matches; the default requires exactly one. A regexp only.
           replace  only outside <index> and <query>: the stored name is rewritten (the default keeps the name and
                    adds the form to the index). The query word keeps its own form and gets the new one.
           common   a plain word from and its to are common words: they do not count as unmatched words of a name.
                    Not in <index>.
           enabled  "false" removes the rule that the upper layers define with this object, from and to.
         The key of a rule is object, from and to. A rule of a lower layer with the key of an upper rule replaces
         it, whatever the place of both; two rules with one key in one file are an error. To change the to of an
         inherited rule, disable it and add the new one. -->
</rules>
